course/modules/M/M3/L09.html:552:            <div class="explain" hidden><p>\(D^{-1/2}\) has entries \(1/\sqrt{b_{ii}}\). A PSD matrix can have a zero diagonal entry, for example \(\operatorname{diag}(0, 1)\), and then \(D^{-1/2}\) does not exist; Proposition 3.5 shows that the whole row is then zero, so the hypothesis is needed.</p></div>
course/modules/M/M3/L07.html:75:        <p><em>(a).</em> The matrix \(G\) is symmetric, since \((A^{\top}A)^{\top} = A^{\top}A\). For \(x \ne 0\), \(x^{\top}Gx = \|Ax\|^2 > 0\), because \(Ax = 0\) would be a nontrivial combination of the independent columns. For an eigenvalue \(\lambda\) of \(G\) with unit eigenvector \(v\), \(\lambda = v^{\top}Gv = \|Av\|^2 > 0\). The spectral theorem (Lesson 4) gives \(G = V\Lambda V^{\top}\) with \(V\) orthogonal and \(\Lambda = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_k)\), where every \(\lambda_j > 0\). Then \(G \cdot V\Lambda^{-1}V^{\top} = V V^{\top} = I\), using the fact that \(VV^{\top} = I\) for an orthogonal \(V\) (Fact 1.2 of Lesson 6). So \(G\) is invertible.</p>
course/modules/M/M3/L07.html:227:            <p>The matrix \(P = \operatorname{diag}(1, 0)\) above satisfies \(P^2 = P\) and has no inverse. If \(P\) were invertible, multiplying \(P^2 = P\) on the left by \(P^{-1}\) would give \(P = I\), which is false for that \(P\).</p>
course/modules/M/M3/L08.html:72:        <p>For the matrix \(M\) of Proposition 1.1, \(\operatorname{tr}(M) = \dfrac{1}{T - 1}\sum_{t=1}^{T} \|\tilde x_t\|^2\).</p>
course/modules/M/M3/L08.html:82:        <p>Let \(M = V\Lambda V^{\top}\) be a spectral decomposition, with \(V\) orthogonal, \(\Lambda = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_n)\), and \(\lambda_1 \ge \lambda_2 \ge \cdots \ge \lambda_n\). Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, such a decomposition exists for every real symmetric matrix (the spectral theorem, Theorem 2.3 of that lesson). <dfn id="def-principal-component-analysis" data-concept="principal-component-analysis">Principal component analysis</dfn> of the data \(X\) consists of three objects. The <em>principal directions</em> are the columns \(v_1, \ldots, v_n\) of \(V\). The <em>score</em> of observation \(t\) on component \(k\) is \(z_{tk} = \tilde x_t^{\top} v_k\). The <em>explained share</em> of component \(k\) is \(s_k = \lambda_k / \sum_{j} \lambda_j\), which is defined when \(\sum_j \lambda_j > 0\).</p>
course/modules/M/M3/L08.html:325:            <p>Let \(X\) have rows \((1, 2)\), \((3, 4)\) and \((5, 9)\). Compute \(\tilde X\), the matrix \(M\), and \(\operatorname{tr}(M)\). Check the trace against Proposition 1.2, and find the determinant of \(M\).</p>
